Such is the strange, security-conscious, cracker-ridden world in
which we live and compute.  Recent unices support execute-only
binaries that the operating system can execute on your behalf, but
that you can't read on your own.

There are ways to run certain executables (notably setuid shells
or setuid programs with shell escapes) under a debugger and trick
them into giving you root access.  If you can't read the
executable, neither can your debugger.  I'm sure someone else can
post or point you to the details.

